	"peak": 1783,
	"summary": {
		"text": "A loose confederacy of Bariba and Busa kingdoms led by Bussa, its sacred seat, Nikki, its centre of power, and Illo, its market, with Kaiama, Parakou, Kandi, Kouande and others beside them. Its ruling warrior class, the Wasangari, traced their kings to the hero Kisra, a legend that probably remembers horsemen who conquered the country in the 15th century and that set Borgu apart from its Muslim neighbours. Borgu withstood Songhai and Nupe, sheltered Oyo's exiled kings, then spent two centuries as Oyo's vassal before winning its freedom in 1783. Its army died with Oyo's at Ilorin in 1837, and in 1898 Britain and France split it along today's Nigeria–Benin border.",
		"sources": [
			"wikipedia-borgu",
			"wikipedia-kisra-legend"
		],
